= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Baire category theorem> states that in a complete <metric space>, a <countable> intersection of open <dense> sets is <dense>. Equivalently, a nonempty <complete metric space> cannot be a <countable union> of <nowhere dense sets>, where a set is <nowhere dense> if its closure has empty interior.

Here is the proof. Let $G_1,G_2,\ldots$ be open <dense> sets and let $U$ be any nonempty <open set>. Choose a point $a_1\in U\cap G_1$ and a radius $0<r_1<1/2$ such that the <closed ball> $B_1=\overline B(a_1,r_1)$ lies inside $U\cap G_1$. Inductively choose $a_{m+1}$ in the nonempty <open set> $B(a_m,r_m)\cap G_{m+1}$, and then a radius $0<r_{m+1}<2^{-m-1}$ such that
$$
B_{m+1}=\overline B(a_{m+1},r_{m+1})\subseteq B(a_m,r_m)\cap G_{m+1}.
$$
Thus the <closed> balls are nested and their radii tend to zero. For $j,k\geq m$, both centers lie in $B_m$, so $d(a_j,a_k)\leq2r_m$. They form a <Cauchy sequence>. <Completeness> gives a limit $a$, and closedness puts it in every $B_m$. Consequently $a\in U\cap\bigcap_mG_m$. As $U$ was arbitrary, **the intersection is <dense>**.

To obtain the equivalent formulation, take complements of the closures of the <nowhere dense> sets; those complements are open <dense>. Conversely, complements of open <dense> sets are <closed> with empty interior. In particular, a <countable> <closed> cover of a nonempty <complete metric space> must contain a set with nonempty interior.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For each nonzero <integer> tuple $k=(k_1,\ldots,k_{n+1})$, put
$$
H_k=\left\{x\in\mathbb R^n:\sum_{j=1}^nk_jx_j=k_{n+1}\right\}.
$$
If its first $n$ coordinates are all zero, then $k_{n+1}\ne0$ and $H_k$ is empty. Otherwise it is a <closed> proper <affine hyperplane>. It has empty interior: from any of its points, an arbitrarily small displacement in the nonzero normal direction $(k_1,\ldots,k_n)$ leaves it. Thus every $H_k$ is <nowhere dense>.

There are countably many <integer> tuples, and $\mathbb R^n$ is complete. The <Baire category theorem> therefore makes the complement of their union <dense> and nonempty. Choosing any point there gives
$$
\boxed{\sum_{j=1}^nk_jx_j\ne k_{n+1}\quad\text{for every nonzero integer tuple }k.}
$$
Equivalently, $1,x_1,\ldots,x_n$ are <linearly independent> over $\mathbb Q$: multiplying a rational relation by a common denominator produces just such an <integer> relation. This is an instance of <Baire avoidance of countably many affine hyperplanes>.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use $\mathbb T=\mathbb R/(2\pi\mathbb Z)$ and normalized measure $dt/(2\pi)$. The <Fourier partial sum> at zero is the real <linear functional>
$$
L_Nf=S_N(f,0)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(t)D_N(t)\,dt,
$$
where the <Dirichlet kernel> is
$$
D_N(t)=\sum_{j=-N}^Ne^{ijt}=\frac{\sin((N+\tfrac12)t)}{\sin(t/2)}.
$$
The quotient follows by summing the finite <geometric progression>, with its removable value $D_N(0)=2N+1$. It is a real <continuous> <periodic function>. If
$$
\Lambda_N=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}|D_N(t)|\,dt,
$$
then $|L_Nf|\leq\Lambda_N\|f\|_\infty$. This bound is its exact <operator norm> even on the real <continuous> functions. Indeed, for $\delta>0$ set
$$
f_{N,\delta}(t)=\frac{D_N(t)}{\sqrt{D_N(t)^2+\delta^2}}.
$$
It is real, <continuous> and periodic with <norm> at most one, and
$$
L_Nf_{N,\delta}=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}
\frac{D_N(t)^2}{\sqrt{D_N(t)^2+\delta^2}}\,dt\longrightarrow\Lambda_N
$$
by <dominated convergence>. At zeros of the kernel the integrand is zero, and elsewhere its limit is $|D_N|$.

For $0<t\leq\pi$, $\sin(t/2)\leq t/2$. Using evenness and putting $v=(N+1/2)t$ gives
$$
\Lambda_N\geq\frac2\pi\int_0^{(N+1/2)\pi}\frac{|\sin v|}{v}\,dv
\geq\frac4{\pi^2}\sum_{j=1}^N\frac1j.
$$
For the last inequality, split into the first $N$ complete intervals $[(j-1)\pi,j\pi]$: their sine integrals are two and $1/v\geq1/(j\pi)$. The <harmonic sum> tends to infinity. This proves the <Dirichlet kernel harmonic lower bound>.

Choose $N$ with $\Lambda_N>K$, then choose $\delta$ sufficiently small that $L_Nf_{N,\delta}>K$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\|f_{N,\delta}\|_\infty\leq1,\qquad |S_N(f_{N,\delta},0)|>K.}
$$
This constructs <continuous> near-maximizers, rather than using the discontinuous sign of the kernel as the desired function.

= iv
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The real space $C(\mathbb T)$ is a <Banach space> in the <uniform norm>: a uniformly <Cauchy sequence> has a uniform pointwise limit by <completeness> of $\mathbb R$, and that <uniform limit> is <continuous>. Each functional $L_N$ from the preceding part is bounded, but their norms are unbounded.

For integers $m\geq1$, define
$$
E_m=\{f\in C(\mathbb T):|L_Nf|\leq m\text{ for every }N\}.
$$
Each $E_m$ is <closed>, being an intersection of inverse images of <closed> intervals under <continuous> functionals. It has empty interior. Otherwise some ball $B(f_0,r)$ would be contained in it. For every $h$ of <norm> at most one, both $f_0$ and $f_0+(r/2)h$ would belong to that ball, so
$$
\frac r2|L_Nh|\leq |L_N(f_0+(r/2)h)|+|L_Nf_0|\leq2m.
$$
Taking the <supremum> over $h$ would give $\|L_N\|\leq4m/r$ for all $N$, contradicting the preceding part.

Thus the $E_m$ are <nowhere dense>. The <Baire category theorem> says their union cannot cover $C(\mathbb T)$. A function outside that union satisfies
$$
\boxed{\sup_N|S_N(f,0)|=\infty,}
$$
so its <Fourier series> diverges at zero. In fact these functions form a <dense> $G_\delta$ subset of $C(\mathbb T)$. This proves the relevant <uniform boundedness principle> argument directly and establishes <generic unbounded Fourier sums at a fixed point>.

= 2
{parent=Paper 6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The hypotheses make $p$ a <sublinear functional> and $q$ a <superlinear functional>. In particular $p(0)=q(0)=0$. Apply the domination property to $y+y'\in Y$ and the vector $x+x'$:
$$
S(y)+S(y')\leq p(x+x'+y+y')-q(x+x').
$$
<Subadditivity> of $p$, with the inserted vectors $z$ and $-z$, gives
$$
p(x+x'+y+y')\leq p(x+y+z)+p(x'+y'-z).
$$
Superadditivity of $q$ gives $q(x+x')\geq q(x)+q(x')$. Combining these inequalities and moving terms yields exactly
$$
\boxed{S(y')-p(x'+y'-z)+q(x')
\leq-S(y)+p(x+y+z)-q(x).}
$$
Every vector appearing in the comparison is permitted by the assumed domination, so no membership of $x,x',z$ in $Y$ is needed.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

We prove the extension by <Zorn's lemma>, retaining the full domination condition. Consider all pairs $(Y,S)$ extending $(Y_0,T_0)$, with $Y$ a subspace and $S$ linear, satisfying $S(y)\leq p(x+y)-q(x)$ for every $x\in X,y\in Y$. Order these pairs by extension. A chain has an upper bound: take the union of its subspaces and maps. The union is a subspace, and its map is well defined and linear because any finitely many vectors lie together in one member of the chain. Its domination is inherited there. Thus a maximal extension exists.

Suppose its domain $Y$ is proper, and choose $z\notin Y$. A linear extension to $Y+\mathbb Rz$ has the form $S_a(y+tz)=S(y)+ta$. Define the <admissible values for a sandwich extension> by
$$
A=\sup_{x',y'}\{S(y')-p(x'+y'-z)+q(x')\},
$$
$$
B=\inf_{x,y}\{-S(y)+p(x+y+z)-q(x)\},
$$
with $x,x'\in X$ and $y,y'\in Y$. Part (i) shows every quantity in the <supremum> is at most every quantity in the <infimum>, hence $A\leq B$. Both bounds are real and finite: choosing zero vectors provides $A\geq-p(-z)$ and $B\leq p(z)$, and the pairwise comparison then bounds them from the other sides. Choose $a\in[A,B]$.

For $t>0$, <positive homogeneity> and the upper bound on $a$, used with $x/t,y/t$, give
$$
S(y)+ta\leq t\big[p(x/t+y/t+z)-q(x/t)\big]
=p(x+y+tz)-q(x).
$$
For $t=-s<0$, the lower bound on $a$ applied to $x/s,y/s$ gives
$$
a\geq S(y/s)-p(x/s+y/s-z)+q(x/s),
$$
which rearranges to $S(y)-sa\leq p(x+y-sz)-q(x)$. For $t=0$ this is the existing domination. Thus $S_a$ is a legitimate larger extension, contradicting maximality. The maximal domain is therefore all of $X$; call its map $T$.

Setting the first vector in the domination to zero yields $T(v)\leq p(v)$. Setting it to $v$ and the domain vector to $-v$ yields $-T(v)\leq-q(v)$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{T|_{Y_0}=T_0,\qquad T(y)\leq p(x+y)-q(x),\qquad q(v)\leq T(v)\leq p(v).}
$$
The one-dimensional extension has checked both signs of its coefficient; <positive homogeneity> alone does not permit treating negative coefficients as positive ones.

= iii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Take $Y_0=\{0\}$ and $T_0(0)=0$. The hypothesis $p(x)\geq q(x)$ is exactly the required initial domination $0\leq p(x+0)-q(x)$. Applying the extension proved in part (ii) gives
$$
\boxed{q(x)\leq U(x)\leq p(x)\quad\text{for every }x\in X,}
$$
with $U$ linear. There is no reason that $U$ must be nonzero; for instance $p=q=0$ forces $U=0$.

This is the <sandwich form of the Hahn-Banach theorem>. Conversely, any <linear map> between these two functionals satisfies the stronger mixed domination, since $U(y)=U(x+y)-U(x)\leq p(x+y)-q(x)$. Thus the mixed inequality used during extension is the appropriate invariant, not an extra final restriction.

= 3
{parent=Paper 6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Use a nonzero complex unital <Banach algebra> $B$ with submultiplicative <norm>, and write its <unit> as $e$. The complex <scalar field> is the usual convention in this spectral statement; for a real algebra one uses its complexification. We derive the needed analytic facts from norm-convergent series and scalar complex analysis.

If $\|a\|<1$, <completeness> makes the <Neumann series> $\sum_{j=0}^\infty a^j$ converge. Multiplying its finite partial sums by $e-a$ gives $e-a^{m+1}$, which tends to $e$. Hence its sum is the inverse of $e-a$. If $b$ is invertible, then $b+h=b(e+b^{-1}h)$ is invertible whenever $\|b^{-1}h\|<1$. This proves that the invertible elements form an <open set> and that inversion is locally norm-continuous.

Define the <Banach algebra spectrum> and the <resolvent of an element> by
$$
\sigma(x)=\{\lambda\in\mathbb C:\lambda e-x\text{ is not invertible}\},
\qquad R(\lambda)=(\lambda e-x)^{-1}\quad(\lambda\notin\sigma(x)).
$$
For $|\lambda|>\|x\|$, the <Neumann series> gives
$$
\boxed{R(\lambda)=\sum_{j=0}^\infty\frac{x^j}{\lambda^{j+1}},\qquad x^0=e.}
$$
Thus $\sigma(x)$ is bounded by $\|x\|$ and is <closed>, because invertibility is open. Also $R(\lambda)\to0$ in <norm> at infinity; for example its <norm> is at most $\|e\|/(|\lambda|-\|x\|)$, since $\|e\|\geq1$ in a nonzero submultiplicative unital algebra. The standard normalization $\|e\|=1$ is not needed.

For points where the <Banach algebra resolvent> exists $\lambda,\mu$, multiply out the inverses to get the <resolvent identity>
$$
\boxed{R(\lambda)-R(\mu)=(\mu-\lambda)R(\lambda)R(\mu).}
$$
For $\lambda_0$ outside the <Banach algebra spectrum>, put $R_0=R(\lambda_0)$. Locally,
$$
R(\lambda_0+h)=R_0(e+hR_0)^{-1}
=\sum_{j=0}^\infty(-h)^jR_0^{j+1},\qquad |h|\|R_0\|<1.
$$
This series and its differentiated series converge uniformly on every smaller disk. It therefore proves $R$ is a <Banach-space-valued holomorphic function>, establishing complex differentiability in the Banach-space <norm> and gives $R'=-R^2$, without assuming a Banach-algebra-valued analytic theorem. In particular every <continuous> complex <linear functional> $\phi$ makes $\phi(R(\lambda))$ a scalar <holomorphic function>.

The <Banach algebra spectrum> is nonempty. Otherwise $R$ would be defined and analytic everywhere. Its local <norm> continuity and decay at infinity make it globally bounded. For every <continuous linear functional> $\phi$, the scalar <entire function> $\phi(R(\lambda))$ is bounded and tends to zero at infinity, so it is identically zero: the scalar <Cauchy estimate> for its <derivative> on circles of radius $r$ is $M/r$, which tends to zero, and the limiting value then fixes its constant as zero. Such functionals separate points by the <Hahn-Banach theorem>. Explicitly a bounded real functional can be extended from the real span of any nonzero element using its <norm>, and complexifying it by $\phi(v)=\ell(v)-i\ell(iv)$ gives a bounded complex <linear functional> nonzero on that element. Therefore $R$ itself would be zero, contradicting $(\lambda e-x)R=e$. We have proved **the <Banach algebra spectrum> is a nonempty compact subset of the <complex plane>**.

We next establish the <spectral radius formula>. The power norms are submultiplicative. If some $x^m=0$, their root norms are eventually zero. Otherwise, for any fixed $m$, write $n=qm+r$ with $0\leq r<m$ to obtain
$$
\|x^n\|\leq\|x^m\|^q\max_{0\leq r<m}\|x^r\|.
$$
Taking roots gives $\limsup_n\|x^n\|^{1/n}\leq\|x^m\|^{1/m}$. Taking the <infimum> in $m$, and using that every root <norm> is at least that <infimum>, proves existence of
$$
\rho_0=\lim_{n\to\infty}\|x^n\|^{1/n}
=\inf_{m\geq1}\|x^m\|^{1/m}.
$$
For $|\lambda|>\rho_0$, the <root test> makes $\sum x^j/\lambda^{j+1}$ converge, even when $|\lambda|\leq\|x\|$. The same telescoping <multiplication> proves it is a two-sided inverse. Hence
$$
r_\sigma:=\max_{\lambda\in\sigma(x)}|\lambda|\leq\rho_0.
$$
For the reverse bound fix $R>r_\sigma$. If $R_1>\max(R,\|x\|)$, integrate the uniformly convergent exterior series term by term on $|\lambda|=R_1$, obtaining
$$
x^n=\frac1{2\pi i}\int_{|\lambda|=R_1}\lambda^nR(\lambda)\,d\lambda.
$$
The same integral equals the one on $|\lambda|=R$: applying an arbitrary <continuous linear functional> reduces <contour deformation> through the <Banach algebra spectrum>-free annulus to the scalar <Cauchy theorem>, and separation of points restores equality in $B$. This proves the <resolvent Cauchy coefficient formula> rather than assuming vector-valued analytic calculus. Using the <norm> bound for the allowed vector integral gives
$$
\|x^n\|\leq R^{n+1}\max_{|\lambda|=R}\|R(\lambda)\|.
$$
Taking $n$th roots yields $\rho_0\leq R$, and letting $R\downarrow r_\sigma$ finishes the proof:
$$
\boxed{\rho(x)=\lim_{n\to\infty}\|x^n\|^{1/n}
=\max_{\lambda\in\sigma(x)}|\lambda|
=\sup\{|\lambda|:\lambda e-x\text{ is not invertible}\}.}
$$

For the first example take the <commutative algebra> of <dual numbers> $B=\mathbb C[\varepsilon]/(\varepsilon^2)$ with <norm> $\|a+b\varepsilon\|=|a|+|b|$. <Completeness> follows from finite dimension, and the product estimate makes the <norm> submultiplicative. For $x=\varepsilon$, $x\ne0$ but $x^2=0$, so $\rho(x)=0$. Directly, $\lambda e-\varepsilon$ has inverse $\lambda^{-1}e+\lambda^{-2}\varepsilon$ for $\lambda\ne0$, and its <Banach algebra spectrum> is $\{0\}$. For the second example take $B=\mathbb C$ with its absolute-value <norm> and $x=1$. Its <Banach algebra spectrum> is $\{1\}$, giving **$\rho(x)=\|x\|=1$**.

= 4
{parent=Paper 6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

We construct the finite evaluation representation explicitly by <Lagrange interpolation>. This supplies all the <polynomial> facts needed for the bound.

Define the <Chebyshev polynomial> by $T_0=1,T_1=x$ and
$$
T_{m+1}(x)=2xT_m(x)-T_{m-1}(x).
$$
Induction shows that $T_m$ has degree $m$; the <cosine> addition identity also proves $T_m(\cos\theta)=\cos(m\theta)$. Therefore $\|T_n\|_{[-1,1]}=1$, and $T_n(-x)=(-1)^nT_n(x)$ follows from the recurrence. The case $n=0$ is immediate, since both $P$ and $T_0$ are constant.

For $n\geq1$, choose the descending extremal nodes
$$
x_j=\cos(j\pi/n),\qquad 0\leq j\leq n,
$$
so $T_n(x_j)=(-1)^j$. Define the fundamental interpolation polynomials
$$
\ell_j(t)=\prod_{k\ne j}\frac{t-x_k}{x_j-x_k}.
$$
They satisfy $\ell_j(x_k)=\delta_{jk}$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{P(t)=\sum_{j=0}^nP(x_j)\ell_j(t)}
$$
for every <polynomial> of degree at most $n$: the difference has degree at most $n$ and vanishes at the $n+1$ distinct nodes, so it is zero. The root count used here follows by successively factoring $(t-x_j)$ from a <polynomial> having such a root; a nonzero degree-$n$ <polynomial> cannot have more than $n$ distinct roots. Thus the interpolation identity has been proved.

If $u>1$, every numerator factor in $\ell_j(u)$ is positive, while exactly $j$ denominator factors are negative. Hence $\operatorname{sgn}\ell_j(u)=(-1)^j$. Applying interpolation to $T_n$ itself gives
$$
\sum_{j=0}^n|\ell_j(u)|=\sum_{j=0}^n(-1)^j\ell_j(u)=T_n(u)>0.
$$
Putting $M=\sup_{[-1,1]}|P|$ in the interpolation formula yields
$$
|P(u)|\leq\sum_{j=0}^n|P(x_j)||\ell_j(u)|\leq M T_n(u).
$$
For $u<-1$, apply the proved positive-side inequality to $Q(t)=P(-t)$ at $-u>1$, and use the parity identity. The result is
$$
\boxed{|P(u)|\leq\|P\|_{[-1,1]}|T_n(u)|,\qquad u\notin[-1,1].}
$$
This is sharp, with equality for $P=T_n$ or its scalar multiples.

In functional terms, the evaluation map $E_u:P\mapsto P(u)$ on the normed <polynomial> space has $\|E_u\|=|T_n(u)|$: the inequality gives the upper bound and $T_n$ attains it. For $u>1$, its normalized version has the exact representation
$$
\frac{E_u(P)}{T_n(u)}=\sum_{j=0}^n\lambda_jP(x_j),
\qquad\lambda_j=\frac{\ell_j(u)}{T_n(u)},\quad
\sum_{j=0}^n|\lambda_j|=1.
$$
Thus <Chebyshev interpolation represents external evaluation> using $n+1$ nodes. The necessary finite representation and its <norm> are derived directly, so no convex-hull theorem is being invoked without proof.
